ed for transparent huge pages (`MADV_HUGEPAGE`), so it faults in 2 MiB at a time, with one TLB entry each: a segment is two huge pages (4 MiB, aligned to its size), a span segment sixteen, and a span keeps its segment's advice when freed and taken again. On Trig checking supabase-studio on pup, huge pages for span segments and for the segments of every heap, where those kept small pages, run 3.3% faster at one checker, 4.9% at four, and 3.5% balanced, with 41 to 67% fewer cycles walking page tables and 36 to 58% fewer faults. Their cost is peak memory, 2.5 to 3.4% higher on a large project and 6 to 8% on a tiny one, as a huge page commits 2 MiB of a List's room not yet filled, or of a segment its heap has not written, as a server's worker's or a blocking-pool thread's that keeps to one segment (huge pages for large blocks alone, and for the segments of heaps that fault in ahead alone: docs/history/records/2026-10/alloc-large/, alloc-thp16/, and alloc-seg-thp/). Every request for huge pages is made only while `/proc/buddyinfo` shows enough free blocks of 2 MiB or more outside the `DMA` zone on the node of the thread that asks (`freeHugeNow`, one cold function, which counts none when it cannot read the file, and on a host of several nodes counts only the lines of the node `getcpu` names, as a fault compacts on its own node before it takes another's, every node's where that node has none, and none where the reading filled its 4 KiB and may have lost that node's lines; a reading answers for 4 ms (`keptReading`), so a burst of Tables, 43 for each of Trig's checkers, reads it once, while a segment or large block may ask only on a reading taken then, whose count its own blocks are taken from): under the default `defrag` of `madvise` a fault in an advised range with no free 2 MiB block compacts memory in the fault, mostly in vain, and on memory fragmented so Trig ran 34 to 41% slower than with no huge pages (docs/history/records/2026-10/perf-stage/thp-fragmented.md). A rese